Raw dev notes -- A Whirlwind of Features: From Reader Engagement to AI-Powered Writing

**Project Background:**
This project is all about building out a robust knowledge base, specifically a platform for publishing articles and fostering community engagement around them. The core idea is a RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) pipeline, meaning we're leveraging AI to help authors create content and potentially enhance the reader experience by drawing from a rich, curated knowledge base. It's a blend of traditional publishing with cutting-edge AI, aiming to make deep knowledge accessible and engaging.

**What was done:**
This past week felt like a sprint across the entire application stack. On the reader-facing side, we pushed hard on engagement features. I implemented a full suite of sharing options (WhatsApp, Telegram, X, copy link) for each article, added a floating WhatsApp group CTA, and built out discovery features like a "surprise me" random article button and a "recommended for you" strip based on reading history. Gamification got a boost too, with monthly read counts and series progress tracking. We also added a simple one-tap poll asking "Did this article help you?" and spent a good chunk of time refining comment styling – clearer quotes, better threading, and responsive fixes for mobile. The Table of Contents also got a redesign, moving to the right and revealing on scroll.

But the biggest chunk of work, and honestly the most exciting, was on the writer experience. We're building a "guided article writer" that's deeply integrated with AI. This involved a massive redesign of the editor canvas, simplifying it, adding RTL polish, and integrating a live SEO rail, quality checklist, and a publish gate. The AI assist is now wired up to our Gemini model (though still using a demo key for now, waiting on the real one!). We've got a brief-first flow, persistent formatting tools, and even mockups for quick-search and fact-checking. Towards the end of the week, I started on Phase 3: the AI self-edit review stage, complete with real fact-checking and search capabilities, and a cool feature to highlight specific sentences for review. We also introduced a genre picker, making the AI assistance genre-aware, and refined how authors define "refuted" claims.

Under the hood, I fixed a critical syntax error that had silently disabled *all* comment JavaScript – classic. I also implemented `mtime` cache-busting and improved our content sync process to detect edits to existing posts, not just new ones. This led to a necessary backfill of about a dozen edited articles. And for a practical touch, I added a one-click PDF export feature for articles and their approved comments, with an option for the reader to include comments or not, using `mPDF` server-side. Finally, I prototyped a per-block B2 tagging strategy for our RAG corpus, which resulted in a 29% smaller corpus while preserving identity – a big win for efficiency.

**What was hard or surprising:**
That comment JS bug was a real head-scratcher. It was a tiny syntax error, but it completely nuked a core piece of functionality without much fanfare. Debugging that felt like finding a needle in a haystack. Integrating the AI assist, especially getting the Gemini API wired up and handling the different phases of the guided writer, was complex. There are so many moving parts: the brief, the scaffolding, the SEO, the quality checks, and now the self-edit review. Each phase has its own logic and UI considerations. The `mPDF` integration for PDF export also had its quirks, as always, but we got it working server-side which is much cleaner. The corpus optimization was a pleasant surprise – seeing a 29% reduction from a relatively simple change felt like a big win for future scalability and cost.

**Personal reflection:**
This week felt like a marathon, but a really satisfying one. I jumped between frontend UX, backend logic, and AI integration, which is exactly the kind of varied work I thrive on. There were moments of frustration, especially with that JS bug, but the feeling of seeing the guided writer come to life, even in its early stages, is incredibly rewarding. It's exciting to build tools that genuinely empower content creators. I'm learning so much about designing intuitive workflows and integrating complex AI capabilities into a user-friendly interface. It really reinforces the idea that good software isn't just about features, but about how those features make people *feel* and how they enable them to do their best work. It's a constant balance between technical implementation and user empathy.

**What's next:**
Polish the writer experience, ship the PDF export, and finalize the watcher edit-detection.
